F2 (BLOCK) — `RULE_IDS` is missing `link.dead`, and behaviour 7's harvest is
by construction blind to the call shape that emits it. One missing id, two
call sites, one regex shape.

  packages/cli/src/check.ts:450   checkLink("link.dead", where, ev as string, `gate "${gid}" evidence`);
  packages/cli/src/check.ts:617   else checkLink("link.dead", where, href, "digest evidence");

`checkLink` (check.ts:372) forwards to `add(rule, "block", where, ...)`
(check.ts:376), so `checkStudio` can and does emit `link.dead`. Observed live
on a copy of the real officina:

  $ bisellium check $S/a4/studio --level block
    link.dead   acta/2026-09-17-kickoff.md   digest evidence "../README.md" not found under studio (dead link)

`link.dead` is absent from packages/cli/src/rules/ids.ts. Both new rules
therefore misjudge it:

  (a) FALSE BLOCK. A lesson honestly stamped with a real rule id is blocked:
      $ # lessons/L-001.md: addressed_by: "link.dead"
      $ bisellium check $S/a4/studio --level block
        lesson.addressed_by  lessons/L-001.md  addressed_by "link.dead" names no opus, rule id, or decision

  (b) FALSE ADVISORY. A lex clause citing it is called nonexistent:
      $ # leges/qa.md §3 clause gains "(check: link.dead)"
      $ bisellium check $S/a4/studio | grep marker_unknown
        lex.marker_unknown  leges/design.md  1 clause(s) cite a rule that does not exist: docs.arch.stale
        lex.marker_unknown  leges/qa.md      1 clause(s) cite a rule that does not exist: link.dead

Behaviour 7 passes anyway because the harvest regex the brief specified
(briefs/W-035.md:133, copied verbatim into rules/lex.test.ts:25) captures only
`add("literal"` and `rule: "literal"`. `checkLink("link.dead", ...)` is a
third shape. The guard is under-inclusive, so "RULE_IDS equals the harvest"
is true and still wrong. The brief calls its regex "the two shapes
`checkStudio` actually uses" — there are three.

Census method, so the next round does not have to guess: I enumerated every
double-quoted dotted literal matching the rule-id shape in check.ts plus
every rules/*.ts (excluding ids.ts and *.test.ts), filtered out filenames,
and diffed against RULE_IDS. Exactly one id came back — `link.dead`, at the
two sites above. Independently, I re-ran the brief's own harvest by hand:
123 ids over 8 files, equal to the registry, confirming the registry is
complete *with respect to the under-inclusive regex*. I also cross-checked
every rule id emitted live by `check studio` and `check examples/sample-studio`
against RULE_IDS: 18 distinct ids, all present. No `add(<variable>)` call
sites exist except check.ts:376 (the `checkLink` forwarder itself).

